<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Senusret II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">19th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a> · Also known as Sesostris II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Senusret II was a king of the Twelfth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the nineteenth century BCE. He began the large-scale development of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a>, controlling the waters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahr-yusuf">Bahr Yusuf</a>, and built his pyramid at el-Lahun with the planned town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-lahun">Kahun</a> for its workers and priests. The tomb of the nomarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khnumhotep-ii">Khnumhotep II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beni-hasan">Beni Hasan</a> dates to his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/senusret-ii.jpg" alt="Senusret II" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArchaiOptix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Upper_part_of_portrait_statue_of_pharaoh_Sesostris_II_with_nemes_and_ritual_beard_01_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Senusret II, also called Sesostris II, was the fourth king of the 12th Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-ii">Amenemhat II</a>. His mother is identified as Queen Senet, on the strength of her titles King's Daughter, King's Wife and King's Mother, although this is not certain. A double-dated stela at Konosso, the stela of Hapu, supports a co-regency in which Amenemhat II appointed his son co-ruler, and many Egyptologists accept co-regencies in this dynasty, though Claude Obsomer rejects them. His throne name was Khakheperre, meaning The Ka of Re comes into being.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The length of the reign is disputed. The Turin Canon is believed to give Senusret II nineteen years, but the latest attested regnal year is his eighth, and some scholars prefer a reign of nine or ten years. Others, such as Jürgen von Beckerath and Frank Yurco, keep the longer figure because of the scale of the building he undertook.</p><p>He took a great interest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a>, a region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> that had been inhabited for more than 8,000 years. There he built a dike at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-lahun">El-Lahun</a> and a network of canals drawing water from Lake Moeris, in order to reclaim land for farming and to exploit the marshes for hunting and fishing. His successors continued the work, which matured under his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>. He moved the royal necropolis from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a> to El-Lahun, where he built his pyramid, and the nearby town of Senusrethotep, now Kahun, is the first known workers' quarter.</p><p>His reign was a time of peace, with no recorded military campaigns and growing trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. The tomb paintings of the official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khnumhotep-ii">Khnumhotep II</a> show visiting Western Asiatic foreigners labelled Aamu, among them a leader named Abisha, the first known use of the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Senusret II was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a>, born to Khenemetneferhedjet I. Whether the two shared a co-regency is debated: the majority view favours three years, but the only evidence is a scarab bearing both names, and Murnane argued that any co-regency lasted no more than a few months.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Senusret II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The pyramid town at el-Lahun (Kahun), built for the cult of <b>Senusret II</b>, preserves a planned settlement of officials and workers, together with papyri on accounts, medicine, veterinary care and household lists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
